<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Purushanda</h1><p class="st-meta">2000 BCE – 1700 BCE (approx.) · capital Purushanda</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Purushanda, an Anatolian city whose ruler bore the title great king in the age of the Assyrian merchants, later humbled by Anitta of Kussara.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Burushattum appears in Old Assyrian trade letters of the 20th and 19th centuries BCE as a wealthy city in south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, probably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acemhoyuk">Acemhöyük</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksaray">Aksaray</a>, though the identification is debated. Assyrian merchants bought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and wool there and kept a trading house.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its ruler was the only Anatolian king the merchants called great king, suggesting overlordship over neighbouring cities. Later Mesopotamian legend made it the target of a campaign by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> to protect merchants, a story told in the text known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-battle">King of Battle</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 18th century BCE the king of Purushanda submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anitta">Anitta of Kussara</a>, offering him an iron throne and iron sceptre. The city then lost its independence and fades from the record by c. 1700 BCE.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 2000 BCE – 1700 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:19.90%;width:5.97%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2000 BCE – 1700 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Purushanda</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anatolian religion</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Burushattum</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1850&amp;focus=purushanda" aria-label="Purushanda on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5521 798 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Purushanda lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5841 1054L5927 1023"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5927 1023h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5841 1054h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5841 1054h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-1850&amp;focus=purushanda">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

